{
  "$schema": "https://appflare.github.io/catalog/schema/v1.json",
  "slug": "deepcrawl",
  "name": "Deepcrawl",
  "summary": "An open-source Firecrawl alternative: an API that turns any web page into clean Markdown, metadata and a tree of its links for AI agents, cached in KV with request logs in D1.",
  "tagline": "Turn web pages into clean text and organized links",
  "homepage": "https://deepcrawl.dev",
  "repo": "lumpinif/deepcrawl",
  "license": "MIT",
  "categories": ["developer-tools", "ai"],
  "authors": [{ "name": "Felix Lu", "github": "lumpinif", "x": "felixlu1018" }],
  "maintainers": ["MendyLanda"],
  // Upstream's release tags (deepcrawl@X.Y.Z) version the project's
  // TypeScript SDK, the package named deepcrawl, which is released together
  // with the API Worker; the Worker's own package carries no version. So the
  // version comes from install.version, and the bump bot, which reads no
  // deepcrawl@ prefix as a version, cannot move this pin: move source to the
  // newest deepcrawl@ tag and install.version with it by hand.
  "source": { "ref": "deepcrawl@0.5.5", "sha": "c40e8e567b7d85b36bf45aa6b5d828249e1831ba" },
  "install": {
    "tier": "artifact",
    "packageManager": "pnpm",
    // The API Worker alone. Upstream's full platform adds a Better Auth Worker
    // and a Next.js dashboard with a Postgres database; the API also runs
    // without them, checking a signed token on each request (AUTH_MODE jwt),
    // which is how this entry installs it.
    "wranglerConfig": "apps/workers/v0/wrangler.jsonc",
    "workerName": "deepcrawl",
    "version": "0.5.5",
    // No build: wrangler bundles src/index.ts and the workspace packages it
    // imports, which ship as TypeScript source.
    // The config binds upstream's Better Auth Worker (AUTH_WORKER), which is
    // not part of this entry; the API treats the binding as optional and
    // reads it only with AUTH_MODE better-auth. The vars left out point at
    // upstream's dashboard and auth origins on localhost and name upstream's
    // own GitHub and Google OAuth clients; the API reads the first two only to
    // allow cookie sessions from them, and the OAuth client ids not at all.
    // No upstream pull request yet.
    "configPatch": {
      "services": null,
      "vars": {
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL": null,
        "BETTER_AUTH_URL": null,
        "GITHUB_CLIENT_ID": null,
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID": null
      }
    }
    // Every path, / included, answers 401 without a token in jwt mode, which
    // the health check counts as the Worker answering.
  },
  // Two KV namespaces (the read and links caches) and a D1 database (request
  // logs), all with free tiers. Pages are fetched with plain fetch: no Browser
  // Rendering or Workers AI.
  "plan": "free",
  "requires": [],
  "secrets": [
    {
      "name": "JWT_SECRET",
      "label": "Token signing secret",
      "help": "Clients sign their API tokens with it (HS256). Setting a new value makes every token issued so far invalid.",
      "generate": true
    },
    {
      "name": "UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL",
      "label": "Upstash Redis REST URL",
      "help": "Turns on per-client rate limiting of the API, with the token below. Without both, requests are not rate limited.",
      "optional": true
    },
    {
      "name": "UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN",
      "label": "Upstash Redis REST token",
      "help": "The REST token of the Upstash database above.",
      "optional": true
    }
  ],
  "vars": [
    {
      // Upstream's config sets AUTH_MODE to better-auth, which needs the auth
      // Worker this entry leaves out, and a patch may not change a var, so the
      // entry sets jwt here.
      "name": "AUTH_MODE",
      "label": "Authentication mode",
      "help": "Keep jwt: the API then accepts only tokens signed with the token signing secret. The other modes need upstream's auth Worker or leave the API open to anyone.",
      "default": "jwt",
      "required": true
    },
    {
      "name": "JWT_ISSUER",
      "label": "Token issuer",
      "help": "When set, a token's iss claim must equal it. Leave empty to accept any issuer.",
      "default": ""
    },
    {
      "name": "JWT_AUDIENCE",
      "label": "Token audience",
      "help": "When set, a token's aud claim must include it. Leave empty to accept any audience.",
      "default": ""
    },
    {
      "name": "API_URL",
      "label": "API URL",
      "help": "Where clients reach the API, shown as the server in the OpenAPI document.",
      "default": "{{workerUrl}}"
    },
    {
      "name": "WORKER_NODE_ENV",
      "label": "Runtime mode",
      "help": "Development writes debug logs and returns detailed errors to callers; keep production unless you are debugging.",
      "type": "select",
      "options": [
        { "value": "production", "label": "Production" },
        { "value": "development", "label": "Development" }
      ],
      "default": "production"
    }
  ],
  "postInstall": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "Every request needs `Authorization: Bearer <token>`: a JWT signed with HS256 and the **Token signing secret** from this app's settings. It must carry a `sub` claim (any id for the caller) and should carry an `exp`; with a token issuer or audience set, `iss` and `aud` must match. There is no sign-in page and no dashboard: whoever holds the secret mints tokens, with any JWT library or with upstream's script from a checkout of the repository:\n\n```sh\npnpm jwt:mint -- --secret <token signing secret> --sub me --expires-in 24\n```"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "Then read a page as Markdown:\n\n```sh\ncurl '{{workerUrl}}/read?url=https://example.com' -H 'Authorization: Bearer <token>'\n```\n\n`POST /read` returns the full result with metadata, and `/links` the page's link tree. The API reference is at {{workerUrl}}/docs and the OpenAPI document at {{workerUrl}}/openapi, both behind the same token. Point the `deepcrawl` SDK at {{workerUrl}} with the token as its API key."
    }
  ],
  "tokenPermissions": []
}
